Java 17 - Sealed Classes

Definition:
  -> Introduced in Java 17.
  -> Used to control inheritance.
  -> Allows only specified classes/interfaces to extend or implement a class/interface.
  -> Prevents unauthorized subclasses at compile time.

Syntax:

public sealed class Payment
        permits UPI, Cash, CreditCard {
}


Why Sealed Classes?

Problem without Sealed Classes

Any class can extend a parent class.

class Payment {}

class UPI extends Payment {}
class Cash extends Payment {}
class CreditCard extends Payment {}
class Crypto extends Payment {}    // Allowed (Not desired)

Suppose only UPI, Cash, and CreditCard should be valid payment methods.

Need controlled inheritance.



Why not use final?
public final class Payment {}

Result:
No class can extend Payment.
 UPI
 Cash
 CreditCard
 Crypto

Issue:
  -> Stops inheritance completely.
  -> Cannot allow selected child classes.


Why Package-Private Doesn't Help?

Making Payment package-private:

class Payment {}

Effects:
  -> Cannot extend outside package.
  -> Cannot even access Payment outside package.

This restricts access, not just inheritance.



Solution - Sealed Class
public sealed class Payment
        permits UPI, Cash, CreditCard {
}

Only these classes can extend Payment.

final class UPI extends Payment {}
final class Cash extends Payment {}
final class CreditCard extends Payment {}


Attempting:

class Crypto extends Payment {}

Compilation Error: Crypto is not allowed in the sealed hierarchy.

Rules of Sealed Classes: 
  
Rule 1) Every permitted subclass must declare one modifier:
        -> final
        -> sealed
        -> non-sealed

Otherwise compilation fails.

All sealed class subclasses must either be
final, sealed or non-sealed.


Rule 2) Every permitted subclass must explicitly extend the sealed class.

Example

sealed class Payment
        permits Cash {}

final class Cash {
}

Compilation Error

Because Cash is permitted but does not extend Payment.

Correct:

final class Cash extends Payment {}

Rule 3) Permitted subclasses must belong to the same package/module as the sealed class.

Example

payment
    Payment

payment2
    Cash

Compilation Error:
Class payment2.Cash from another package
not allowed to extend sealed class Payment


Three Possible Modifiers

1. final
Stops inheritance completely.

final class CreditCard extends Payment {}

Cannot extend further.

class HDFCCreditCard extends CreditCard {}

Compilation Error

Use When : Hierarchy should end.


2. sealed
Continue controlled inheritance.

sealed class CreditCard
    extends Payment
    permits AmexCard {
}

final class AmexCard extends CreditCard {}

Only AmexCard is allowed.


3. non-sealed : Removes restriction.

non-sealed class UPI
        extends Payment {
}

Now any class can extend UPI.

class GPay extends UPI {}
class PhonePe extends UPI {}
class Paytm extends UPI {}

Unlimited inheritance.



Hierarchy Example
                 Payment (sealed)
              /        |         \
             /         |          \
      CreditCard    Cash      UPI
        (sealed)    (final)  (non-sealed)
            |
        AmexCard(final)

UPI
 ├── GPay
 ├── PhonePe
 └── Paytm



When to use Each Modifier:

1) final
    -> No further inheritance.
    -> End of hierarchy.

2) sealed
    -> Continue controlled inheritance.
    -> Only selected subclasses allowed.

3) non-sealed
    -> Open inheritance.
    -> Anyone can extend.
